21:40:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your body does use glucose as an important fuel, especially for the brain and during exercise—but not all food “turns into the same stuff” in the same way. - **Carbohydrates** are most directly broken down into glucose. Some raise blood sugar quickly; others are digested slowly because of fiber and food structure. - **Fats** are mostly broken into fatty acids. Many tissues can burn these directly for energy, and the liver can make ketones from them. Some fats are essential because your body cannot make them. - **Protein** is broken into amino acids, which are mainly needed to build and repair muscles, enzymes, hormones, immune cells, and more. The body *can* turn some amino acids into glucose if needed, but that is not their primary job—and there are essential amino acids you must get from food. Food also supplies things that cannot simply be replaced by glucose: **vitamins, minerals, fiber, essential fats, and essential amino acids**. Glucose has calories, but it does not provide iron, vitamin C, calcium, omega-3 fats, etc. Even when foods provide the same number of calories, they can affect the body differently. For example, a sugary drink and a meal containing beans, vegetables, protein, and fat may eventually provide usable energy, but they differ in digestion speed, fullness, blood-sugar response, gut health, nutrient content, and how likely you are to eat more soon afterward. So the goal is not merely to get glucose. It is to provide energy *plus* the raw materials and regulatory signals the body needs to function well.
